        "problem": "Solve for $q$ where ${25}^{q+1}={125}^{q}.$",
        "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $25={5}^{2}$ and $125={5}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{2})}^{q+1}={({5}^{3})}^{q}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2(q+1)}={5}^{3q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+1) & =3q \\\\ 2q+2 & =3q \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
